# InterGenOS shell blur-effect defaults.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL.
# The image enables the blur extension for every new user
# (config/gsettings/91_intergenos-extensions.gschema.override lists
# blur-my-shell@aunetx in enabled-extensions), and until this file the image
# shipped NO settings for it whatsoever. Every visual and structural default of
# a default-enabled component was therefore whatever the third-party bundle
# happened to ship, and could change under us on any version bump with nothing
# in the tree stating what we intended. Declaring the defaults we rely on is the
# point of this file; the two keys set below are the ones with a measured
# structural consequence.
#
# WHY dconf AND NOT A gschema OVERRIDE (the same reason as 00-intergenos-dock
# beside this file): the extension ships its gschemas INSIDE its own bundle dir
# and reads its own compiled schema at runtime via Extension.getSettings(), so a
# system gschema override in /usr/share/glib-2.0/schemas cannot reach it. The
# dconf layer is path-based and schema-independent, which makes a system db the
# only mechanism that can seed these.
#
# NOT LOCKED, DELIBERATELY. There is no lock file for these paths. A user who
# wants the static variant sets it and their user db wins, exactly as a default
# should behave. These are defaults, not policy.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT static-blur ACTUALLY DOES, read from the shipped component sources
# rather than inferred from the key name (blur-my-shell 'components/panel.js'
# and 'components/dash_to_dock.js', both read at
# /usr/share/gnome-shell/extensions/blur-my-shell@aunetx on an installed
# system):
#
#   let background_group = new Meta.BackgroundGroup(
#       { name: 'bms-panel-backgroundgroup', width: 0, height: 0 }   # panel.js:183
#   );
#   let static_blur = this.settings.panel.STATIC_BLUR;
#   if (static_blur) {
#       background = pipeline.create_background_with_effects(
#           monitor.index, bg_manager_list,                          # panel.js:196
#           background_group, 'bms-panel-blurred-widget');
#       bg_manager = bg_manager_list[0];
#   } else {
#       const pipeline = new DummyPipeline(...);                     # no monitor index,
#       [background, bg_manager] = pipeline                          # no bg_manager_list
#           .create_background_with_effect(background_group, ...);
#   }
#
# So with static blur ON, each blurred surface creates a REAL background manager
# bound to a MONITOR INDEX, inside an actor group deliberately constructed at
# width 0 / height 0. With it OFF, the same surface gets a live blur effect with
# no background manager and no monitor binding at all. The blur is present
# either way — this selects the implementation, it does not remove the feature.
#
# WHY THAT BINDING IS THE PROBLEM. The monitor index is captured when the
# surface is first blurred, which on a machine with several GPUs and several
# displays happens while the monitor set is still being assembled. A background
# manager bound to an index from that moment does not follow a later change to
# the monitor set. It matches the one recovery that is known to work on the
# affected machine — re-applying the whole display configuration, which forces
# every background manager to be rebuilt — and equally matches the one that is
# known NOT to work: changing the wallpaper setting alone, which re-reads the
# image without rebuilding any monitor binding.
#
# WHAT WAS MEASURED (2026-08-06, from two full journal captures on the affected
# three-GPU machine, both boots that showed the fault):
#   * every offscreen-effect allocation failure in either boot names one of
#     these zero-sized blur groups or the widget inside it, thousands of times:
#     'bms-dash-backgroundgroup [MetaBackgroundGroup] ... needs an allocation'
#     and 'bms-dash-blurred-widget [StWidget] ... needs an allocation';
#   * the two boots fail with the two different shapes the same degenerate
#     allocation produces — 'Failed to create texture 2d due to size/format
#     constraints' on one, "cogl_framebuffer_set_viewport: assertion
#     'width > 0 && height > 0' failed" on the other.
#
# WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. No log line in either capture records a wallpaper paint
# failure, so the step from these allocation failures to a desktop that falls
# back to its flat primary colour is NOT established by measurement. This file
# removes a monitor-index binding that should not be created during display
# settling; that is worth doing on its own terms, and it is not proof of a cure.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

[org/gnome/shell/extensions/blur-my-shell/panel]
# The panel is blurred on the default image, because the image ships the stock
# top-panel layout and enables the extension. This is the one surface where the
# binding above is created for every user on every login.
static-blur=false

[org/gnome/shell/extensions/blur-my-shell/dash-to-dock]
# Not on the default image — the dock layout is opt-in through the welcome
# application — but it takes the identical code path in dash_to_dock.js (the
# same zero-sized group at line 304, the same monitor.index call at line 316),
# so it is pinned here rather than left to be rediscovered by whoever turns the
# dock on. This is the surface that produced the measured failures, because the
# machine they were measured on has the dock enabled.
static-blur=false
